WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.340
Pennywise is returning, and the Losers' Club
might not like it, but film critics are pretty

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.340
psyched.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:11.700
It: Chapter Two has been screened for professional
reviewers, and most are in agreement that

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:17.369
the flick is a worthy follow-up to 2017's
smash hit It: Chapter One, and that together,

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:22.040
the two movies represent one of the better
Stephen King adaptations ever put to film.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.540
Of course, given some of the King adaptations
over the years, that's not a particularly

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:27.640
high bar.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:40.560
King's 1986 novel It tells the story of a
group of outcast youths who must confront

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.629
the evil plaguing their small town of Derry,
Maine, one which appears most often in the

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:47.920
guise of Pennywise, the Dancing Clown.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.820
During a battle in the sewers below the streets
of Derry, the "Losers' Club" manages to severely

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.460
wound It and drive It back into hibernation,
but they fail to finish the job.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:04.040
When It returns over a quarter century later,
It's all too eager for a rematch with the

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.770
now-adult Losers, and it's got a couple new
tricks up its sleeve.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.000
"At this point, anything is possible, so yes:
pies, seltzer bottles, balloon animals...be

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:13.760
alert."

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.740
Bill Skarsgård was praised far and wide for
his performance as Pennywise in Chapter One,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.320
and that trend continued with the second installment,
with many critics noting that director Andy

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.400
Muschietti's decision to give Pennywise less
screen time actually served to make him even

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.240
scarier.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.840
Brian Truitt of USA Today wrote:

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.100
"Andy Muschietti deploys Pennywise sparingly;
it's a smart decision, because it makes the

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:44.299
villain's appearances special, and Skarsgård
finds new ways to totally freak you out, even

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.520
in a surprisingly human fashion."

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.780
Katie Smith-Wong of Flick Feast agreed, praising
the performances of the entire cast while

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:54.170
singling out Skarsgård in particular:

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:59.350
"Overall, It: Chapter Two reminds audiences
that horror film sequels can be good, with

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.760
Skarsgård reinforcing Pennywise's position
as the scariest clown ever."

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.860
The entire cast was on the receiving end of
plenty of critical kudos, but none quite so

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.540
much as Bill Hader, the Saturday Night Live
vet who has proven adept at folding a little

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.300
darkness into his hilarious persona with his
HBO series Barry.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.420
Bill Goodykoontz's review in Arizona Republic
stated:

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:27.290
"Everyone in the cast is good, [James] Ransone
as the hypochondriac Eddie, who never ceases

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.599
complaining, is welcome comic relief.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.860
But Hader rises above the rest.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.920
If you've seen Barry… [then] you know he
has no problem blending comedy and drama.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.600
In this case, he adds horror to the mix, and
he's just as good at that, too."

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:46.220
But with a novel as thematically dense and
challenging as the source material, a crackerjack

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.220
cast will get you nowhere without an expertly
crafted script and assured direction, and

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.720
fortunately, Muschietti and screenwriter Gary
Dauberman came through.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.319
Digital Spy's Ian Sandwell wrote the movie
might make anyone fear clowns:

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:05.590
"Muschietti has managed to craft a series
of chilling set pieces designed to give everyone

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.560
a touch of coulrophobia.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.300
While there's nothing in the sequel that a
die-hard horror fan won't have seen before,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.560
the set pieces are so effectively done that
they'll have an impact regardless."

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.959
Alex Godrey of Empire also gushed over the
dedication to craft apparent throughout the

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:22.740
sequel, saying:

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:29.120
"A psychologically merciless sequel, everything
here is as it should be: deeper, scarier,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:29.860
funnier.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:35.540
Muschietti in particular has stepped up, skilfully
guiding us through a rollicking funhouse.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.860
It is obscenely entertaining."

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.690
Of course, not every film can please every
critic, and even some of It: Chapter Two's

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.460
positive reviews said that it may have been
a bit of a letdown from the first film.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.280
The film's beefy run time at just under three
hours was a sticking point for some reviewers,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.390
including Entertainment Weekly's Leah Greenblatt,
who wrote:

00:03:55.390 --> 00:04:00.670
"The main problem with Chapter Two is that
it goes on, and on, for so very long.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.680
If brevity is not necessarily the soul of
a good scare, it would certainly serve a story

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.920
that sends in the clowns, and then lets them
just stay there, leering and lurking and chewing

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.680
through scene after scene, until the there's
nothing left to do but laugh, or leave."

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.600
Still others, like A.V.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.950
Club's Katie Rife, faulted the flick for its
perceived failure to deliver the one thing

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:26.720
that would have made up for having your butt
glued to theater seat for the better part

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.580
of three hours: actual scares.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:35.080
"The film isn't an abject failure by any means;
it has some funny jokes, a couple of really

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.300
good performances, impressive creature and
set design, and pleasing cinematography.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.400
But when it comes down to it, It: Chapter
Two just isn't all that scary."

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.700
The flick's naysayers, though, were firmly
in the minority camp, and by most accounts,

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:54.610
Muschietti and his cast and crew have stepped
up to deliver a thrilling one-two punch, a

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.120
fitting take on what many fans consider to
be King's greatest work.

